body:
Looking at the spec example from "Introduction to Readable Streams",

``` javascript
function makeSocketStream(host, port, { highWaterMark = 16 * 1024 } = {}) {
  const rawSocket = createRawSocketObject(host, port);

  return new ReadableStream({
    start(push, close, error) {
      // When adapting a push source, usually most of the work happens in start.

      rawSocket.ondata = chunk => {
        if (!push(chunk)) {
          // If push returns false, the internal buffer is full, so propagate
          // the backpressure signal to the underlying source.
          rawSocket.readStop();
        }
      };

      rawSocket.onend = close;

      rawSocket.onerror = error;
    },

    pull() {
      // This is called if the internal buffer has been emptied, but the
      // stream's consumer still wants more data.
      rawSocket.readStart();
    },

    cancel() {
      // If the stream is prematurely canceled, release resources.
      rawSocket.readStop();
      rawSocket = null;
    },

    strategy: new LengthBufferingStrategy({ highWaterMark })
  });
}
```

I guess its assumed that `rawSocket` won't start emmiting data events immediately? It waits till `.readStart()` is called? I know `createRawSocketObject` is a fictitious example, but maybe this could be documented a little more. Will `pull()` be initially called after `start()`?

In that case I'm assuming `createRawSocketObject` does have mechanisms for buffer control. But what if you are wrapping a stream that doesn't.

An attempt to polyfill a WebSocket stream. 

``` javascript
function makeWebSocketStream(host) {
  var ws = new WebSocket(host);

  return new ReadableStream({
    function start(push, close, error) {
      ws.onmessage = function(event) {
        if (!push(event.data)) {
          // ???
        };
      }
      ws.onend = close;
      ws.onerror = error;
    },

    function pull() {
      // ???
    },

    function cancel() {
      ws.close();
      ws = null;
    }
  });
}
```

